# Minecraft Version(s): 26.1–26.3
## 1.66.1
- Quick rounds: no gold ring on the start point any more (it looked like a second entrance; the entrances are the way in)
- Colour floor: players gather on the middle platform, not where /game create was typed (also for colour floors made before)

## 1.66.0
- Mazes: up to 256 cells per side (513 × 513 blocks); built a couple of thousand columns per tick, so a big maze appears over a few seconds without freezing the server (no runs while it's built)
- Mazes: several wall and floor blocks, mixed at random; a light level for the paths (0 = dark, up to 15: an invisible light in every cell)
- Block settings take block tags and lists everywhere: parkour blocks, maze walls and floors, colour floor colours (a tag gives a colour per block; more than nine: nine at random each time), claim border markers (mixed along the border), e.g. minecraft:stone_bricks, #minecraft:wool

## 1.65.0
- Games and dungeons: Nice Mob Manager leaves their mobs alone: every mob a game or dungeon spawns, every entity saved with a dungeon or room (tagged as it's placed), and everything else in the dungeon dimension carries mob_manager.settings.exclude
- Games and dungeons: mobs with data-driven variants (cows, pigs, chickens, cats, wolves, frogs, zombie nautiluses) get a random look and sound from everything the server has, packs like Nice Mob Variants included, never an "ender" one; a mob line that sets its own variant or sound keeps it (before, game mobs always got the default look)

## 1.64.1
- Claims: /claim admin bypass also skips game areas: no inventory put aside, no kit, no game mode switch, no death limits, market/inbox/pets work. Turning it on inside one gives your things back as if you'd left

## 1.64.0
- Games: spectator areas can be a box: /game edit <name> spectate corner1 / corner2 (looked at, given, or at your feet; height included). Players waiting or watching may move anywhere inside it (else within the radius as before); without a spectate point the box's middle is where they're put. spectate clear removes all of it

## 1.63.0
- Games: points per game (panel): a fixed amount when a score counts, plus so many per score (per jump, wave, ghast, point, head…); race, dropper and maze give the fixed amount for finishing; head hunts give per head and the fixed amount for finding all
- Commands in games, dungeons and Rewards rules can do sums: {score*2}, {score/10}, {jumps*3}, {wave+5}, {found*10}, {checkpoint*2}, dungeons {seconds} and {deaths} (whole numbers, rounded), e.g. points give {player} {score*2}

## 1.62.1
- Games: creating a wave fight or quick round sets its spawn (where players appear) where you stand, instead of a separate start point; /game edit <name> spawn moves both. Games made before without a spawn take that spot as their spawn

## 1.62.0
- Games: a colour per game (panel): the entrance's title and particles, the watch point (a lighter shade), the title when a quick round starts and the action bar during parkour, race, dropper and maze runs; gold without one

## 1.61.0
- Wave fights, dungeons and colour floor: peaceful and neutral mobs attack players too (a switch per arena and per dungeon, on by default). Neutral ones (iron golems, llamas, bees, wolves, polar bears, pandas, endermen…) are hostile right away and stay angry; mobs that shoot already (llamas, snow golems) shoot; fliers and water mobs without an attack (parrots, bats, allays, fish, squid) spit from a distance; everything else charges in, with its own bite or swing when it has one (else 1.5 hearts, tougher each wave)

## 1.60.0
- Games: entrances, watch points and back points, placed anywhere (e.g. in the hub) with /game edit <name> entrance|watch|back add|remove|clear. Each has particles and a floating label: entrances show the game's name, what it is, who's playing and the top 3, and stepping in for a second takes players to the game (solo games start the run, wave games bring the party to the start point, quick rounds join the next round); watch points take players to the spectator area; back points are the way out of spectator areas (inside a wave game or quick round, 5 seconds on one leaves it, as before). Spectator areas, dungeon pads and dungeon waiting areas get labels too
- Games: after a game players go to the game hub spawn (/game spawn set), else back where they came from; the start point (formerly start pad) is just where a game begins
- Games: display names with spaces (/game edit <name> title Sky Run, or the panel); commands keep the one-word name
- Parkour: optional box (/game edit <name> corner1 / corner2): lanes share it and the jumps turn at its walls
- Parkour: a reward every … jumps (commands with {jumps}) and the Rewards trigger "Makes jumps in parkour"; the old finish commands are "when a run ends" (they fell)
- Parkour: gets harder in levels (shown on screen, title at each new level), with presets easy, normal, hard, extreme: up and down and diagonal jumps, longer gaps, slabs, then glass panes and iron bars; only jumps a sprinting player can make
- Race: commands at each checkpoint ({checkpoint})

## 1.59.0
- (in 1.60.0)

## 1.58.0
- Game claims: choose the game mode inside (panel claim editor, or /claim admin gamemode <mode> standing in it): adventure (still the default), survival (left as it is), creative or spectator. Survival players switch on entering and are back in survival on leaving (no fall damage after spectator or creative); admins who were in another mode are left alone; moving between game claims with different modes switches straight over. Choosing a mode alone makes a server claim a game area

## 1.57.1
- Points: their name keeps its capitals as written in the panel ("5 Cloud Coins", "1 Cloud Coin")

## 1.57.0
- Points: their name (set in the panel) is now used everywhere players see them, with an optional singular ("Ticket") and abbreviation ("T") for tight spots: shop prices and the action bar. The shop NPCs' line under their name says what they take ("Spend your tickets"); NPCs refresh when the name changes
- Health check of the last rounds (spotlight, quick rounds, head hunt, points, reward shops, waiting areas): no bugs found; a clearer message when a panel-made reward gives nothing yet

## 1.56.0
- Points: one balance per player for all games (named in the panel, e.g. "Tickets"). Earned through Rewards rules with the new reward "Points" (a fixed amount, plus some per score for game triggers), so any game, dungeon, wave, head hunt or event can give points, real rewards or both; /reward points <players> <amount>
- Points commands: /points (balance and what it was for), /points top; admins and command blocks: /points give <players> <n> [reason], /points take (fails when they don't have enough, so a conditional command block after it only runs once they've paid), /points check (succeeds when they have at least that many)
- Reward shops: NPCs (mannequins with the shop's own skin and name) players right-click to spend points. Several shops, each with its own rewards (items, commands, and the Rewards' non-item rewards like flight, pets and perks), price, times per player and times in all; what doesn't fit goes to the inbox. /points shop create|delete|npc add|npc remove|skin|item|open. The NPCs can't be hurt, pushed, put in boats or moved, and come back by themselves when they go missing; they look at players close by
- Spectator areas for every game that waits: quick rounds with a spectator area (/game edit <name> spectate [radius]) let players who step on the pad during a round queue there for the next one (its join window opens for them when the round ends), and players who are out watch the rest from there. Dungeons: /dungeon pad wait <pool> [radius] sets a pool's waiting area; when every copy is in use, a party queues there and starts as soon as one is free

## 1.55.0
- Games: quick rounds, five new templates. The first player on the start pad opens a join window (10 s); everyone who steps on it plays the same round, each for their own score (solo works too). Nobody dies in a round: a deadly hit knocks them out, healed, nothing dropped. Kit or own gear, exit pads, /game try for a round with only you
  - Ghast deflect (ghasts): hit the ghasts' fireballs back with snowballs (handed out and refilled); a ghast killed by one counts. Default ghasts carry ExplosionPower:0 so the arena stays whole
  - Colour floor (colorfloor): /game edit <name> grid builds a 3×3 grid of coloured platforms (colours, size and gap set in the panel). A colour is called and vanishes after a few seconds; after a few colours the grid refills and it gets faster; a mob from the pool every few colours. Last one standing wins
  - Anvil rain (anvils): anvils fall from the top of the play box, more and more (half of them near a player); landed ones vanish. Last one standing wins
  - Treasure grab (treasure): glowing items appear on the box's floor (item=points, rarer the more they're worth); walk into them
  - Fishing frenzy (fishing): catches score points (fish, rare fish, treasure, junk) instead of landing
- Games: head hunts (heads). Player heads with the hunt's own skin (/game edit <name> texture <url or value>) hidden in the world: /game edit <name> headitem gives heads to hide (placing one adds it), head add|remove for a head you look at. Players right-click them (also inside claims); a reward every few heads (commands with {found}/{total}, and the new Rewards trigger "Finds hidden heads"), finding all of them runs the finish commands. Players can't break them; an admin in creative can. /game join <hunt> shows how far you are
- Rewards: new trigger "Finds hidden heads (head hunt)" (every … heads)

## 1.54.0
- Market spotlight (reward): shines on one listing per player instead of all of them. Players pick which one: "★ Put in spotlight" on their listing in /market → My listings, or "Spotlight" on the website profile; until they pick, it's on their newest listing
- Market: spotlit listings stand out more in game: a gold "In the spotlight" section on the market's front page (up to 3, a fair shuffle), a gold banner over them at the top of Browse with a divider before the rest, gold names and a ★
- Fix: /reward spotlight <player> off now takes the star off the listing (it stayed when nobody else had a perk)
- Removed the own join message reward (/joinmessage and /reward joinmessage); reward rules lose that reward on load (a rule that only gave a join message goes away)

## 1.53.0
- Per-dimension tuning: "Always clear sky": players in that dimension never get rain or thunder from the server's weather, so its sky doesn't darken while it rains elsewhere (the weather itself carries on). On for the dungeon dimension and in the Flying Fair minigame preset

## 1.52.3
- Claims: switching to spectator and back inside a claim no longer shows its title again (spectators keep the claim they were in)

## 1.52.2
- Claims: players in a claim with its title on, and Nice Mob Manager village name markers standing in one, carry the tag ncc.claim_title; Nice Mob Manager (next version) leaves out its village greeting for them, so it doesn't cover the claim's title

## 1.52.1
- Games: chorus fruit doesn't teleport in game claims, dungeon runs, wave games or anywhere in the dungeon dimension (players get a notice; mobs eating it stay put too)

## 1.52.0
- Claims: a description (up to 100 characters) and an optional title: players walking into the claim see its name as a title in the claim's colour, with the description as subtitle (the same claim greets someone again only after a minute). Set in the claim menu's "Name and colour" page, the panel or the website profile; the description also shows on the map, like a waypoint's
- Log: claim edits from the panel say when the description or the title changed

## 1.51.3
- Tab list: players in the dungeon dimension show "Dungeons" in a red copper brown

## 1.51.2
- Dungeons: the dungeon dimension is left out of every tweak and tuning setting and runs with its own light setup instead: no natural spawning at all (only the dungeons' markers spawn mobs), no random ticks, it stops while nobody is in it. Felling, vein mining, bone meal, sapling replanting, spawner pickup, anti-xray, shared sleep, any-shape portals and dispenser placing don't act there; the cleanup tool and auto caps never remove dungeon mobs or loot

## 1.51.1
- Log: every line the mod writes has one format, "[panel] Section: what happened" (e.g. "[panel] Pets: rule First Join gave Rubber Ducky for SteamPhantom"), so the panel shows all of them as Panel entries; lines that had no section got one

## 1.51.0
- Dungeons: random prebuilt rooms. Build a room, save it with /dungeon room create <name>, /dungeon corner2 and /dungeon room save <name> [group]. A spot in a dungeon (a jigsaw block whose target pool names a group) gets a random room of that group, turned and placed so one of its doors (a jigsaw named like the spot's target) faces the spot, like vanilla jigsaw structures; no data packs needed. A filled spot becomes what the room's door turns into (the doorway); a spot no room came to becomes its own "turns into" block (a wall)
- Dungeons, rooms: rooms can have spots of their own (rooms inside rooms, up to a set number of levels and rooms per copy); a room isn't used twice in a copy while others of its group are left; rooms never reach outside the dungeon's box nor overlap each other; markers inside rooms (mob, boss, checkpoint, exit) work and turn with the room
- Dungeons: weights, chances (per room, per group, per spot), group start level and per-copy limits are set in the panel, without saving anything again
- Dungeons: /dungeon tool marker <text>, door <name> and spot <group> <door> [turns into] hand out ready-made structure and jigsaw blocks; /dungeon room group|weight|delete|list; /dungeon save reports the room spots it found; /dungeon try logs the rooms it picked

## 1.50.1
- Games, waves: exit pads take 5 seconds of standing on them (was 2), so nobody leaves by stepping over one

## 1.50.0
- Games: new template "waves": a party of 1–4 starts it from the arena's pad and fights waves of mobs in a box built in the world (/game create waves <name>, then /game edit <name> spawn, corner1 / corner2 [x y z], mobspawn add, kit). Waves are generated from a mob pool (more mobs per wave and per player, tougher each wave, a boss every N waves) or written by hand per wave ("5: husk x6, skeleton x2"); a set number of waves to clear, or endless
- Games, waves: dead players wait in the spectator area (/game edit <name> spectate [radius]) or watch a teammate, and come back at the next wave; the party loses when everyone is down at once or its team lives run out; their things are held, nothing drops
- Games, waves: one game per arena; other parties stepping on the pad queue up (in the spectator area) and start when it's free. Exit pads (/game edit <name> exitpad add) leave early
- Games, waves: rewards after every cleared wave (wave commands with {wave}; Rewards trigger "Clears a wave in a wave arena") and for clearing the game (finish commands, "Finishes a game", "Sets a personal best"); best = most waves cleared. /game try <name> runs wave 1 with nobody in it
- Games and dungeons: box corners can be given as coordinates (/game edit <name> corner2 ~ ~12 ~, /dungeon create <name> [x y z], /dungeon corner2 [x y z]) or are the block you look at, so the upper corner needs no flying; the box shows as particles while it's being set. The box is only as high as its corners
- Claims: the log says which claim and what changed ("Claims: \"Arena\" (server): game area: sent out after 3 deaths, by NiceRon in the panel"); /claim admin respawn set|clear is logged too

## 1.49.2
- Game claims: a death limit per visit ("Sent out after N deaths", 0 = no limit; the count starts over each visit). With deaths left (or a respawn point set) players respawn inside: at the point set with /claim admin respawn set, else where they entered; the last death sends them out
- Game claims: "Keep the kit on death": kit items stay with a player who respawns inside (otherwise they vanish on death)
- Game claims and dungeons: dying no longer sends picked-up items to the inbox. Without "drop where they die" (game claims) they're kept when the player respawns inside (same game claim, or a dungeon life brings them back in) and gone when the death sends them out (hub, home, out of the run, watching, logging out while dead)

## 1.49.1
- Dashboard: pictures with a relative address (pet heads on the player card) are kept across the 2-second refresh instead of loaded again, so they no longer flicker

## 1.49.0
- Games: players in survival play game claims and dungeon runs in adventure mode and are back in survival once they're out (also after a logout or restart); creative and spectators are left alone. Kit items keep their can_break / can_place_on components, so kit tools with them can still mine and place. Inside game claims these components count instead of the claim's Build role (a player in adventure mode may break what the held item's can_break allows and place or use it where can_place_on allows)
- Games: kit items vanish wherever a player keeps them once they're out of the game (inventory, ender chest, inside shulker boxes and bundles); ender chests don't open in game claims with a kit

## 1.48.5
- Log: everything the mod logs while doing something the panel or dashboard asked for (deleting a report, running a command, player actions…) starts with [panel], so the panel shows it as a Panel entry; commands run by games and dungeons are logged as Game or Dungeon, not Dashboard

## 1.48.4
- Rewards: the default "You got: …" line, /reward and /perks lines and the flight messages are gold, not blue (blue stays for messages from the panel and console)

## 1.48.3
- Pets menu: bond levels are gold (not blue); dungeon checkpoint messages are gold too

## 1.48.2
- Inbox: collecting or claiming in an inbox opened with /inbox stays in the inbox (with a line saying what came) instead of jumping to the market
- Website answers (buying, editing and taking back listings) and the server facts point to /inbox instead of the market's mailbox

## 1.48.1
- Claims: interaction entities (graves, hubs, the spawn's buttons) can be clicked either way by anyone again; only cushions need the build role to break

## 1.48.0
- Dungeons: parties (/party invite, accept, leave, kick, list; up to 4): the leader starts from a pad and members within 16 blocks come along
- Dungeons: lives per dungeon and checkpoint markers (a checkpoint anyone reaches is where everyone comes back); out players watch through a teammate who's still in (no flying around), or go back at once
- Dungeons: mob markers (mob:zombie x3 {data}) and boss markers (boss:<id> {data}) spawn when the copy opens; a boss bar follows the bosses' health and every boss dead can clear the dungeon
- Dungeons: gear per dungeon: their things put aside and a kit given (/dungeon kit <name>; items found inside vanish or go to the inbox), or their own gear, which comes back after a death; never a grave in a dungeon
- Dungeons: best clear times per party size (/dungeon top <name>); Rewards triggers "Clears a dungeon" and "Fails a dungeon"; panel endpoints for runs and closing a run
- Games: per arena a cooldown, start and finish messages, commands on start and finish, parkour jumps ahead and longest gap, race time limit, dropper tries and maze wall height
- The inbox is called Inbox everywhere; opened with /inbox it closes instead of going back to the market
- Pets menu: description, bond level and perks each have their own colour

## 1.47.0
- Instanced dungeons, first part: a void dimension nicecontrolcenter:dungeons comes with the mod (appears after a restart); admins mark a box with /dungeon create <name> + /dungeon corner2 (particle outline) or give corners to /dungeon save <name> <from> <to>, and /dungeon save keeps it as 16 × 16 pieces in the world folder (saving again after changes reuses the box)
- Markers are data structure blocks: start and exit work now (checkpoint, mob and boss come next); chests with a loot table roll fresh in every copy
- Pools: /dungeon pool <name> <pool>; pads with /dungeon pad add <pool> (standing on one for two seconds starts a run with a random dungeon of that pool, never the one played last when there's another) and /dungeon pad remove
- Every run gets its own copy in a free slot, pasted a few pieces per tick behind a boss bar; files are read and written off the main thread; reaching an exit clears it, dying, leaving the box, logging out or the time limit ends it; then the copy is wiped and the slot freed (also on start-up, so no copy outlives a restart)
- Players in a run count as in a game area (no market, inbox or pets; the hub spawn takes over); /dungeon leave, /dungeon list; admins /dungeon try <name> (from the console: a copy that stands for a minute), /dungeon runs, /dungeon delete
- Per dungeon (config for now, the panel next): pool, party size, time limit, enter/prepare/cleared/failed messages and commands run on enter and exit

## 1.46.1
- Dashboard: the top tiles keep their widths when values change

## 1.46.0
- Game areas: the market, the inbox and pets don't work inside (menus, /market, /inbox, /pets, the pet bag); pets that are out stay hidden there and come back on leaving
- Hub spawn (/game spawn set|clear): players who log out or die in a game area, or anywhere in the hub's dimension, come back there instead of their home or bed
- Claims: anyone may use (sit on) cushions and click interaction entities; hitting or breaking them needs the build role
- Nice Actions menu: NCC's buttons have item icons, and there's an Inbox button

## 1.45.1
- Game areas: ender chests don't open where things are put aside (no smuggling items in or out); game items (kit or picked up inside) that end up outside their area vanish within a second and can't be picked up there

## 1.45.0
- Mini-game templates (/game): infinite parkour (jumps made ahead of you, harder as you go, each player in their own lane, blocks cleaned up), race course (checkpoints in order; running, boat or elytra), dropper (land in the water at the bottom, back to the top otherwise) and maze (generated, made new daily or after each run); start pads or /game join, /game leave, /game top, /game list; admins: /game create <type> <name>, /game edit … (start, top, finish, checkpoint add|clear, size, build), /game delete
- Personal bests per arena; Rewards triggers "Finishes a game" (at least … jumps / within … seconds) and "Sets a personal best" with {score} and {game}

## 1.44.0
- Per-dimension tuning (Tuning → Per dimension): no natural mob spawning, no random ticks, mobs and block entities frozen while nobody is in the dimension, quicker item despawn; a one-click minigame profile for eden:flying_fair

## 1.43.0
- Game areas (admin options on server claims and their subclaims, for minigames): inventory, armour, off hand, XP and effects put aside on entering and given back on leaving; a kit on entering (/claim admin kit set|clear, or from the panel); items picked up inside vanish on leaving or go to the inbox; on death no grave (keepinv:has_died is blocked there), picked-up items drop or go to the inbox; logging out inside sends the player to their home or respawn point
- No Nice Actions homes, beds or anchors in game areas, and the Nice Actions back teleport (dialog) can't lead into them; a dimension whitelist for Nice Actions homes (Gameplay → World & nature)
- /inbox opens the market mailbox
- Warping Wonders admins (tag wawo.admin) may place waypoint hubs in dimensions where players can't

## 1.42.0
- /reward: one admin command for non-item rewards (also offline players): fly, claimblocks, pet (… remove), petslots, listings, coupons, spotlight, joinmessage, xp, haste, blockboost, bondboost, toolcare, off for each, info, and give <rule> for reward rules. Replaces /claim admin blocks and /pets grant|revoke
- Timed perks run only while they work (online, not AFK; haste only in their claims): XP boost from orbs, Haste in your claims, claim-block boost, pet bond boost, tool care (half wear)
- Shop coupons worth N diamonds off one server-shop purchase (claim blocks, command offers, pets), in game and on the website; whole items, never below 0
- Market spotlight: the seller's listings first with a ★ in /market and on the website; extra market listing slots
- Own join message (/joinmessage <text>) as a reward, also announced to the panel/Discord
- Up to 3 pets out at once (pet slots): extra pets share the owner's move in their own spots and never overlap; "Also out" in /pets and on the website
- Rules: rewards to give (the same list as /reward, shown in {rewards}), an "only in their claims" condition, triggers "Enters one of their claims", "Leaves their claims" and "Given with /reward give" (waits for offline players or until they're in their claims)
- /perks lists what a player has; the panel's player card shows it too

## 1.41.0
- Flight is now a time credit for claims only: /ncc fly <players> <time> adds time (it adds up), /ncc fly <players> off takes it
- Players switch it with /fly inside a claim they own or are at least a Builder in; leaving the claim switches it off. The time only runs while it's on and they're not AFK; without time, /fly says so
- Flights from 1.39/1.40 carry over as credit

## 1.40.0
- /ncc fly <players> <time> [everywhere|claims]: "claims" lets them fly only inside claims they own or are a member of; outside, flight pauses (they float down with Slow Falling) and comes back when they return. Everywhere stays the default

## 1.39.0
- /ncc fly <players> <time>: flight in survival for a while (time like /tick: 100t, 30s, 1d), /ncc fly <players> off; kept over logouts and restarts, warns before it ends and lets the player float down
- Rewards: the time of day as HH:MM and a new in-game date or weekday trigger, both on the Nice Actions clock and calendar when the pack is installed; a trigger that simply repeats every few minutes; {date} variable
- Firework rockets and stars keep their flight duration and explosions for the website market (also listings already up)

## 1.38.0
- Event rewards: commands and a message when a player joins (first time or every time), leaves, enters a dimension, earns an advancement, dies, kills a mob, reaches an XP level or a playtime, or at an in-game time or day; for everyone or one player, every time, once per player or once at all, with a cooldown and variables
- Own commands: besides links, texts (/rules) and aliases (/spawn, with {player} and {args}, optionally with the server's rights), each with an operator level or a permission node
- Shared night: chosen worlds count their players together for the sleeping percentage
- First joins are reported to the panel (welcome in chat and on Discord)
- /ncc heap dump writes a heap dump for memory analysis; /claim admin blocks <player> remove <amount>
- Clean-up: an entity census (every kind in loaded chunks) and removing all of one kind
- Errors & warnings have their own page; more harmless noise recognised (block-break mismatches, "moved wrongly", ender pearls of players logging out, missing optional recipes)
- Longer market listing times now apply to listings already up; more pet reaction lines; the mob cap in the live data; laggiest chunks no longer show "<null>"

## 1.37.0
- Pets on the website (My SMP Profile, through the panel): which pet is out, how pets move, the particle trail, a random pet, perks on/off, reactions, the name above the pet, and each pet's name and colour. Same rules as /pets
- The website's profile shows when the player first joined, and lore and enchantments on their listings

## 1.36.0
- Market listings can be changed on the website (My SMP Profile): price, how many it's for, and the amount. Fewer items go back to the mailbox; more come from the same items in the mailbox, then the inventory while online, in whole lots, and the listing runs its full time again (like "Add more"). Same limits as the menus

## 1.35.0
- Claims can be kept longer: an admin extends how long a claim stays while its owner is away (from the panel); the inactive-players check leaves it alone until then. Server claims and subclaims never expire, as before

## 1.34.0
- For the website's My SMP Profile (through the panel): a player's own waypoint hubs, changing them away from the hub (name, description, colour, public or private, trusted players; same rules as the owner menu, the floating name updates too), their market listings, and taking a listing back (the items go to the mailbox; an online player is told)

## 1.33.0
- /afk for every player: marks you AFK (tab list tag, skipping the night, pet perks and claim block earning treat you as AFK) until you move or look around, or type /afk again. Permission nicecontrolcenter.afk

## 1.32.0
- Dashboard: new Checks tab with errors & warnings, the server settings check and the bloat check (they were the first part of the Server tab, which now opens on server.properties)
- GC tile shows ZGC's background work when that's the bigger number: ZGC hardly ever pauses the server, so "paused" stayed at 0.0% while the collector was busy
- Players: AFK players show an "AFK n min" badge on their card and a yellow dot in the list; the card's overview shows the player's market bank balance, and the Market tab starts with the bank
- Shorter descriptions on several cards

## 1.31.1
- /ncc heap shows what grows: from the second run on, it also lists the classes that grew most since the run before (bytes and objects), which is what points at a memory leak; one run only shows what is big. /ncc heap <name> lists only classes whose name contains it, e.g. /ncc heap pathfinder or /ncc heap zombie

## 1.31.0
- Market bank: /market → Bank to deposit and withdraw the currency (diamonds by default). Purchases (player listings, claim blocks, command listings, pets) take from the bank first, then from what you carry. Sales are paid straight into the seller's bank; earnings already waiting in mailboxes moved into the banks. Withdrawing gives what fits, the rest stays in the bank
- Buying on the website: through the panel, players see their bank and buy with it on the website. Items go to the mailbox, claim blocks and pets are theirs right away, command listings wait in the mailbox with a [ Claim ] label and run where the player stands when claimed

## 1.30.1
- Lighter locator bar (Performance, on by default) now covers the second place the game rebuilds a moving player's whole locator bar: every position change, not just the chunk tracker's move. That was most of the work between ticks in a recording (87 % of it was player movement packets, mostly locator bar checks), up to a third of each second while three players moved around

## 1.30.0
- Craft suspicious sand and gravel (Tweaks, off by default): sand or gravel plus any one item in a crafting grid (table or inventory) gives suspicious sand or gravel holding that item with all its components (enchantments, name, damage, contents, custom data…), named e.g. "Suspicious Sand (Blade)". Brushing it gives the exact item back. Suspicious sand and gravel can't go inside, nothing is handed back from the grid (no empty bucket), and real recipes still win. Replaces the 3,000 per-item recipes Nice Things had (removed in Nice Things 3.10), which lost the item's components. The crafter block doesn't make them

## 1.29.0
- Vein mining: "Also drop XP" switch (on by default, as before). Off, the rest of a mined vein drops no XP orbs; the ore you mine yourself still drops its XP as usual
- Hopper idle wait (Performance): item filters under a hopper line no longer let items slip past. A filter hopper with nothing to move waited 8+ ticks, while a hopper in the line passes each item on after 8, so it often missed it. A waiting hopper now looks again at once when the container above it changes

## 1.28.0
- Updates: data packs have "Install now": the update is downloaded (if it isn't yet) and checked like any update, the old file is backed up (so it can be rolled back), the new one put in and the data packs reloaded, without a restart. Mods are still installed at the next restart
- Data packs added, replaced or removed in the world's datapacks folder while the server runs (by hand, through the panel's files or an update) are loaded on their own with /reload once the folder stays the same for a few seconds; admins are told what changed. New config option datapack_auto_reload (on)

## 1.27.0
- Claims cover enchantments that change blocks: Frost Walker's ice and Enchantments Encore's Magma, Grass and Summer Walker, Plow and Electric Walker (every replace_disk, replace_block and set_block_properties effect) only change blocks in a claim where the wearer is a builder; for a mob wearing them, the claim's mob switch decides. No message (walking over water would warn every step)
- Waypoint hubs can be placed again right where one was just broken: dropped items and XP orbs on the spot (like the old hub's own item) no longer block a hub item, which silently did nothing until they were picked up or despawned; anything else still blocks as for any armor stand
- Claims cover data packs acting for a player: Nice Things' biome brews (fillbiome) and Fabled Roots' bloom scrolls (place feature) leave the biome and blocks in claims that player can't build on as they were, and the player is told once on the action bar. Only commands run for the player by a data pack (advancement rewards, enchantment functions) are checked; commands someone types work as before

## 1.26.1
- /ncc heap: which classes fill the server's memory (class histogram of live objects, like jcmd GC.class_histogram, but from inside the server, so it works without JDK tools in the image); the top 15 in chat, the top 30 in the log. It runs a full garbage collection first, so the server may pause a moment
- Reports: after stopping a recording (or when one ends on its own) the list says "Building the report…" and shows the new report as soon as it's written, instead of only after reloading the page

## 1.26.0
- Tuning → Saving, teleports and locator bar: "Lighter locator bar" (on by default). Vanilla works out a moving player's whole locator bar on every movement packet, checking every mob and player that shows on it each time; now it's done at most every 5 ticks per player, and at once after a teleport or a dimension change. Looks the same in game; much cheaper when a data pack puts mobs on the locator bar
- Tuning → Data pack selectors: "Selectors by type" (on by default). @e[type=villager,…] without a distance used to check every loaded entity each time a data pack ran it; the server now keeps the entities of each type in their own list, in the same order, and only looks at those
- Lighter mob AI also covers mobs with a brain (villagers, copper golems, frogs, allays, piglins…): far from every player their senses and behaviours run only every few ticks, while their memories keep counting down every tick; tamed and owned mobs always think
- Lag stretches are only minutes where the server thread is busy two thirds of each second or more (was 45 %, which flagged ordinary busy evenings), a warning only when TPS really dropped for a minute or a minute was 80 % busy, and the lowest TPS is the worst minute's average (a single second with one long tick read as "TPS 1.0")
- Between ticks: a mod's own task (and Nice Control Center's) shows the calls below its own code, not the server loop above it
- Scheduled block and fluid tick counts are taken every 10 seconds instead of every second

## 1.25.3
- Auto caps no longer log twice (and no longer log "Cleanup: removed … mobs" every run): under the panel only its alert line shows; without the panel one summary line every 10 minutes at most, as before

## 1.25.2
- Waypoint hub owner menu: the Waypoints button opens the list for hubs in the Nether again (the command ran at the player's position divided by 8, so the pack found neither the hub nor the player there and nothing happened)
- Waypoint hub owner menu: when the Waypoints button can't open the pack's list, the owner is told in chat (with the command's error) and the server log notes the hub, player, place and error, instead of nothing happening; "Go back to the hub" also goes to chat now, where it isn't missed after the menu closes

## 1.25.1
- Under the panel, performance changes ("Recovered …, back to normal") and lag alerts are no longer logged by the server as well: the panel's alert line is enough (servers without the panel still log them)

## 1.25.0
- Between ticks: each task (e.g. the player packet handleMovePlayer) shows the call paths inside it that took the time, so a report can tell why handling movement or another packet got slow
- Diagnosis finds lag stretches: minutes where TPS dropped or ticks plus the work between them took most of each second, named with their times, and says whether the ticks or the work between them grew; a window that's fine on average but had such stretches is no longer called "running smoothly"
- Diagnosis notes memory in use (after garbage collection) that keeps growing for half an hour or more while the same players have about the same chunks loaded
- The lag spike finding counts ticks from spike_threshold_ms, like the spike list, instead of twice the tick budget (the two numbers no longer disagree)
- Under the panel, the login check (Discord linking) asks the panel while the login waits instead of on the server thread, so a slow or hanging panel can't freeze the server for everyone when someone joins
- The dashboard's 60 minute view is merged without holding up the server thread

## 1.24.4
- Claims protect named monsters like animals: a nametagged zombie, creeper or other monster in a claim can't be hurt (arrows too), blown up or used by players without the Containers role, so nobody can kill the mob a farm needs; unnamed monsters stay fair game

## 1.24.3
- Better Mending wears the anvil like using it: each repair has a set chance (12 % by default, like vanilla) to chip, damage or break the anvil; never in creative

## 1.24.2
- Market: buying from a player listing checks the price and lot size you saw; if the seller changed them while you had the listing open, nothing is taken and you're shown the new price instead of paying it

## 1.24.1
- Pets move smoothly: their spot and turn (and their name) are sent as keyframes every 3 ticks that players' games glide through over 4, instead of a new spot every tick, which jittered even when the pet barely moved and more so while the server lagged
- Pet animations no longer jump when they start, end or switch: reaction moves fade in and out, spins are whole eased turns that end facing where they started, the head's turn is eased the short way round, and fast wiggles (shiver, shake off, bounce) are slowed down so they show smoothly
- Market and pet menus: the [ Buy ], [ View ], [ Sell ] and [ Switch on/off ] labels come first on their line, so wrapping can't split them into "[" and "Buy ]" any more
- Server shop: buying claim blocks or command listings asks "How many" in a text field, like player listings (up to 64 at once; a wrong number says so)
- Pets in the market: a pet's page says how long each perk takes to unlock ("from bond level 2, about 1 h 30 min out together"); your own pets show how long is left until each perk works, or ✔ when it does

## 1.24.0
- A/B trials removed (/ncc trial and the Reports card)
- Performance presets: five built-ins (Off, Light, Balanced, Farm friendly, Aggressive) that each set every switch and number; saved presets file (config/nicecontrolcenter-performance-presets.json) for the panel
- Pregeneration: with Distant Horizons installed, a job can also pregenerate DH's LODs: after the chunks, DH's own /dh pregen runs over the same area (DH advises against filling LODs by loading chunks fast), its progress shows in the status, stopping the job stops it, and a restart carries on. Without DH the option is ignored

## 1.23.1
- Waypoint hubs: the dimension list is now where hubs are allowed (like claims) instead of where they aren't; until it's saved once in the panel, the old setting keeps working

## 1.23.0
- Monitoring: splits the server thread's busy time into running on a core and waiting (blocked on a lock, waiting for another thread or the disk, or not given a core by the host). New "Server thread" chart, off-CPU numbers in Between ticks, and a "Where it waited" table naming the lock and the thread that holds it
- Monitoring: Machine & threads card with CPU taken by the host (steal), container CPU limit and throttling, I/O wait, pressure (Linux), and CPU per group of threads (C2ME workers, Netty, GC…)
- Diagnosis: "The server thread is waiting, not working", "The host takes CPU away from the server", "The disk is slow to answer" and busy thread groups, each saying whether the fix is on the host or in the game
- A/B trials: /ncc trial start <a> <b> [minutes] [rounds] (and Dashboard → Reports) switches between two sets of performance settings or distances in turns (A B B A …) and gives a verdict that only counts differences bigger than the spread between turns. Sides: current, off, balanced, aggressive, a feature or no-feature, sim6, view8, joined with +. Changes are temporary; lag protection pauses meanwhile
- Performance profiles (Off, Balanced, Aggressive) shared with the panel
- Spawn less often (Performance → Spawning): monsters, water mobs and bats try to spawn every few ticks, each dimension on its own ticks (off by default; on in Balanced and Aggressive)
- Less work on the server thread: pets, claims and market files are written to disk in the background; settings files are checked for changes by a background thread; pet bond progress is saved every 5 minutes and when the player leaves (was every minute)
- Anti-xray remembers each section's hidden copy until a block next to it changes, so chunks sent again cost nothing
- Waypoint teleports: the target's chunks start loading in the background during the warm-up, so the teleport doesn't load them on the spot (with Warm teleport targets on)
- Idle hoppers spread their next look over 4 ticks; panel map data: claims, hubs and dimensions are worked out once a minute (player positions every request); panel data is turned into JSON on the server thread so it can't change while being sent

## 1.22.0
- Pets: nods, flips, dances and other head turns turn the head in place instead of swinging it around its top
- Tab list: {tps} shows the ticks that really ran in the last 5 seconds, so short lag shows up (it stayed at 20 until the average tick went over 50 ms)
- Monitoring: measures what the server does between ticks (player packets, finishing chunk loads, tasks mods queue). That time isn't in the tick times, but the next tick waits for it, so TPS could drop while every tick looked quick. New "Between ticks" card in Performance and a "Work between ticks" finding naming what it was
- Lag protection also counts the work between ticks, so it steps in when TPS drops even though ticks are quick (it only looked at tick times before)

## 1.21.1
- Pets: perk descriptions show the numbers set in the panel (how far, how often, how much) on a pet's page in the shop, in /pets and in the "Perk unlocked" message

## 1.21.0
- Server shop: claim block and command listings with a player head as their icon can have a head texture (set in the panel), shown in /market

## 1.20.1
- Updates: mods and data packs from GitHub are compared by the file's SHA-256 (GitHub's asset digest) instead of its name, so a pack released as one "latest" release whose zip is replaced each time (like A Realm Recrafted) shows its updates. Downloads are checked against that digest; tags without a version number show the upload date

## 1.20.0
- Outer wilds message: players crossing a soft border see an action bar message (with the plop) and, while in the outer wilds, optionally a post effect from the server resource pack (Minecraft 26.3+). Checked every second against the borders set in the panel, so it always matches them; replaces A Realm Recrafted's own fixed check. Text, colours, sound and post effect are set in the panel (World → Borders & reset). Players who join in the outer wilds get the post effect without the message. When the post effect id is changed, players who still have an earlier one saved lose it the next time they join

## 1.19.2
- Waypoint hubs: the owner menu's arrival message switch no longer puts wool under the hub; it switches the message directly. Switched off in the menu, it stays off whatever is under the hub; it can't be switched on while wool is under the hub (the menu says why). Wool the 1.19.1 menu placed is swapped back for the original block when the message is switched on again

## 1.19.1
- Waypoint hubs: the owner menu's "Name & colour" also picks the title colour, from the same colours the dyes give
- Waypoint hubs: the owner menu switches the arrival message (the chat line and sound when someone teleports to your hub) on or off. Off puts wool in the hub's colour under the hub, as Warping Wonders expects; on puts back the block that was there (wool placed by hand drops as an item)

## 1.19.0
- Waypoint hubs (Warping Wonders): hubs can only be placed in the permanent area (inside the soft border), like claims; in the outer wilds the hub item drops again. Uses the borders set in the panel instead of the pack's own fixed check, which Warping Wonders 4.2 drops. Admins in claim bypass mode can place them anywhere
- Waypoint hubs: right-clicking your own hub opens an owner menu: rename it, change its description, make it public or private and trust or untrust players, with a Waypoints button to the usual list. Everyone else still gets the list right away. Both can be switched off in the panel (Claims → Settings → Waypoint hubs), where dimensions without any hubs can be picked too

## 1.18.0
- Pets: 12 new perks. Helpers: XP Magnet (pulls experience orbs from up to 16 blocks), Replanter (replants harvested crops and nether wart, with a seed from your inventory or one that just dropped), Cook (now and then cooks one raw meat, fish or potato in your inventory), Snack Sharer (feeds you the least filling safe food from your inventory when you get hungry), Tinkerer (now and then mends the tool in your hand, else your most worn armor), Lure (fish bite sooner; rod and pet together never past Lure III), Watchful Eye (monsters near you glow, only on your screen), Pack Mule (a bag of 1 to 6 rows, opened from /pets or /pets bag; it belongs to you, so its items stay when the pet or perk goes) and Lucky Paw (now and then one more drop from natural ore and grown crops; never another ore block with Silk Touch). Stronger: Fire Watch (puts you out, not in lava), Soft Paws (takes most of one fall's damage) and Guardian Roar (roars several close monsters away). All of them are tuned in the panel
- Menus: every button in the pets, market and claim menus has a tooltip (what it does, or where Back goes)
- /pets, /claim, /market, /info and the link commands no longer count as needing permissions, so the game runs them from dialog buttons (like Nice Actions' quick actions) and chat links without asking first. Permissions mods can still deny them
- Silk touch spawners keep their mob in the item's own data instead of block entity data, so operators no longer see the "might lead to command execution" warning under them. Spawners picked up before this still place with their mob but keep the warning

## 1.17.2
- Pets: reactions get their own animations. Pets dance to music (and keep dancing while a jukebox plays within 12 blocks), cheer with hops and spins for advancements and level ups, party around their owner for a beaten boss or their birthday, do a flip when you fly or fall, nuzzle you when patted or when you come back, shake off the rain, look around in the dark, nod at a good trade or harvest and shake their head at a breaking tool or a full inventory
- Pets: notes, sparkles, fireworks, hearts and splashes while those animations play

## 1.17.1
- Pets: the name above a pet now moves exactly with it (the pet no longer rides its owner, so both are placed the same way and glide together)
- Pets: 10 new reactions: a totem saving you, beating a monster or a boss (dragon, wither, warden, elder guardian), enchanting, a music disc, ringing a bell, pitch dark, way up high (above y 200), the rain stopping and a raid

## 1.17.0
- Pets: bond levels and perks. A pet's bond grows while it's out with its owner and they aren't AFK; each pet can have up to two perks (set per pet in the panel) that unlock at bond levels from the settings: Lantern, Night Eyes, Fetch (brings what dropped from blocks you broke and mobs you killed; never thrown items), Truffle Nose, Fishing (fish and junk, never treasure), Gardener, Torch Bearer (your own torches), Miner (visible natural ore, and natural stone next to where you're mining; never player-placed blocks), Wood Chopper (the natural tree you started on), Small Gifts, Second Wind, Nipper (picks targets like a wolf; never players, creepers, bosses, named or tamed mobs) and Shield. Perks pause while AFK or in creative mode and respect claims. Players switch single perks off in /pets
- Pets: names. Players name each pet and pick a colour (like claims); the name floats above the pet, hidden while its owner sneaks; owners can hide it altogether in /pets (Name above pet). Admins can clear a name from the player card
- Pets: reactions. Every pet reacts to what happens (eating, sleeping, advancements, snow, the Nether, rain, other pets, a pat: sneak twice while looking at it…) with a move, particles and sometimes a speech bubble, and warns its owner (tool about to break, low health, food or air, a hissing creeper or a mob behind you, fire or lava, inventory full). Players switch reactions off in /pets
- Ore and natural stone placed from now on are remembered per chunk, so the Miner leaves them alone; blocks placed before this version count as natural

## 1.16.2
- Reports: tick several reports (or Select all) to download them as one zip or delete them together
- Tab list: the dimension is shown in front of the player's name instead of after it

## 1.16.1
- /pets: the pet that's out is shown with its head

## 1.16.0
- Server resource pack from the panel: on a server run by the Nice Control Center Panel, the updater keeps a copy of each pack it finds and links players to the panel's copy (named by its SHA-1) instead of GitHub or wherever the pack comes from. Players who can reach the game can reach the pack, and the link and its SHA-1 always match, so a pack published between restarts no longer fails the hash check for everyone joining. The change happens at the next restart like any pack update. Where the pack comes from is kept as the resource pack source, and server.properties gets a fixed resource-pack-id so games replace the old pack instead of keeping each one. Without the panel nothing changes
- /pets: your pets have their own page ("Your pets" button), so the menu's buttons are right there instead of below a long list; the menu shows which pet is out. Take out and Put away keep you on that page

## 1.15.5
- Dispensers play goat horns: the wait is exactly a player's, for the horn's whole cooldown group (all goat horns wait together, Fabled Roots' horns together; a horn's use_cooldown replaces its own time, like for players). With several horns inside, one whose group is still waiting gives way to one that's ready; all of them stay in the dispenser

## 1.15.4
- Gameplay tweak: dispensers play goat horns. A powered dispenser with a horn in it blows the horn and keeps it, as loud and far as a player would, and sculk sensors hear it. Data pack horns (like Fabled Roots' horns) play their own sound. Afterwards the dispenser waits as long as a player would have to (the horn's time or its cooldown), so a redstone clock can't keep it blaring; meanwhile it only clicks. Off by default

## 1.15.3
- Market: item icons show a single item instead of a stack; the amount is in the text next to it
- Market: changing "For how many" to a lot size that doesn't split what's listed (1,700 per 16) offers ways out instead of just refusing: keep that lot size and get the spare items back in your mailbox, or take the nearest lot sizes that fit (each button says the resulting price)
- Market: selling an amount that doesn't split into the lot size rounds it down to the nearest that does (the rest stays with you) and asks to confirm; what was typed stays in the fields after a mistake

## 1.15.2
- Pets: the wandering while their owner is AFK is smooth again. The head no longer leaves the player to be moved block by block (which clients only saw in jumps), and it no longer spins when it reaches a spot: it turns gently, looking where it goes and then at its owner

## 1.15.1
- Dashboard: reports can be deleted (Reports tab), together with the recording they were made from

## 1.15.0
- Smooth saving (on by default): the autosave every 5 minutes no longer writes the whole world in one tick. That tick only notes what changed; chunks are then written a few per tick (at most 1.5 ms of each) and player files one per tick. On the live server that tick caused more than half of all lag spikes (about 190 ms each). save-all, stopping and backups still save everything at once
- Warm teleport targets (on by default): places players teleport to three times in a day (hubs, homes, spawn) stay loaded without ticking, so a teleport there doesn't load the chunks first. At most 24; they show under Loaded chunks as "Teleport target" and can be released there
- Lag protection, reworked: the far-mob percentages of "Tick less where nobody is" and "Lighter mob AI" are now the lowest they go. Without lag mobs tick normally across the whole simulation distance; lag protection's first steps (three quick ones) shrink the ranges down to the set share of the simulation distance at that moment. Then the mob cap and the simulation distance come down taking turns, then how far players see mobs (More steps first), then the view distance. Steps come every 5 seconds instead of 10 while ticks take more than twice the target, and going back up undoes them in reverse, so the far-mob ranges return last. With lag protection off the percentages always apply, as before
- Lag report: spikes from teleports point at /spreadplayers (random teleports), which loads or generates the target chunk on the spot, and at pregeneration
- Pets: particle trails show for their owner again (1.14.0 hid them while the pet was in front of the owner's face); instead they stay close around the head, slightly below and on the side away from the owner
- Dashboard: the overview's charts fill every row; the view and simulation distance chart moved to "More charts"

## 1.14.3
- Market: Browse shows all listings in one scrolling list (pages only past 100)
- Market: rows in lists (Browse, My listings, Mailbox, Sell) show the item without what's inside it (a shulker box's items, a bundle's, a book's pages); the listing's own page still shows everything. 100 full shulker boxes made a 69 MB menu (players get kicked over the client's limit), now 10 KB

## 1.14.2
- Tuning: lower defaults for "Tick less where nobody is" (25 % of the simulation distance for monsters, animals and villagers, 15 % for other mobs) and "Lighter mob AI" (40 %). 1.14.0's 50 % was about twice the old 32 blocks, so mobs far from players were slowed down less than before. Values set in the panel stay as they are

## 1.14.1
- Dashboard: stops asking the server for new numbers while nobody looks (the browser tab is hidden, or the panel shows one of its own pages), and refreshes the 15 and 60 minute views less often; it catches up as soon as it's shown again
- The panel's CPU tile can show the server's CPU in cores (panel 1.13.1)

## 1.14.0
- Gameplay tweak: crops grow faster in the rain (wheat, carrots, potatoes, beetroot, stems, sweet berries, cocoa…), by a percentage set in the panel
- Gameplay tweak: Better Mending. Sneak and right-click an anvil to repair the Mending item in your hand with your experience, at Mending's rate
- Gameplay tweak: AFK players don't count when the game works out how many players need to sleep to skip the night
- Gameplay tweak: hide ores from x-ray (like Paper's anti-xray): ores with no open side are sent to players as stone, deepslate, netherrack or end stone, and shown as soon as a block next to them changes. Off by default; which blocks and below which height are set in the panel
- X-ray hint: fewer false alarms. A diamond hint now also needs few other ores per diamond (cave explorers mine lots of iron, coal and copper on the way), debris isn't judged for players who blast with beds or TNT, and more kinds of stone count as dug
- Claims: anyone may use interaction entities in claims (graves, waypoint hubs, the spawn's buttons); the data packs decide what's allowed
- Tree felling: trees whose branches or leaves touch are no longer felled together; a trunk standing on the ground is another tree (2×2 trunks still fall as one)
- Pets: they no longer fly through their owner during their moves; while the owner is AFK the pet roams around them on its own and goes back to its move when they're back; their particles no longer drift into the owner's view
- Market: selling starts with a row per item you carry, each with its item and a [ Sell ] button, then a form for that item
- Market: change the price of your listing and add more of the item to it (Add more), or add to a listing of the same item straight from the sell form; restocking runs the listing for its full time again
- Market: Browse shows which listings of how many are on the page and has the page buttons below the list too (expensive listings were easy to miss on page 2)
- Tuning: "Tick less where nobody is" and "Lighter mob AI" ranges are a share of the simulation distance now, so they're never farther than it and shrink while lag protection lowers it (at least 16 blocks). Earlier block values are replaced by the new defaults: check them in the panel
- Dashboard: Overview has a View & simulation distance chart; the note under "What's costing performance" lists each setting or check with what it's about; World → Players shows how many chunks are loaded around each player
- Dashboard: the player card is split into tabs (Overview, Items, Places, Claims, Market, Pets, Checks); Claims shows this mod's claims (owned, member of, claim blocks), Market the player's listings with Withdraw, their mailbox and recent trades
- Dashboard: the GC tile shows the number its colour comes from (background work with ZGC), and only warns about it once the heap is filling up too; the Players tile shows the player limit
- Map: waypoint hubs carry their own head and title colour for the panel's map
- API for Merl: market prices per item (MarketApi), for price advice

## 1.13.28
- Gameplay tweak: iron golems when villagers panic. A frightened villager calls an iron golem if none is around, on its own; sleeping, gossiping and other villagers are no longer needed, and gossiping no longer calls golems. Shown on the info page

## 1.13.27
- Market: a player listing's price can be for several items ("For how many" when selling, like 5 Diamonds per 16); buyers buy that many at a time. Older listings stay per item

## 1.13.26
- Market: a listing of more than a stack (like 99 beef) no longer kicks the player who opens its page
- Pets: a player whose connection breaks no longer leaves their pet behind (it was removed off the server thread)

## 1.13.25
- Gameplay tweak: dispensers place blocks in front of them instead of dropping them (also seeds on farmland, redstone dust, string). Items with their own dispenser action work as before, a sulfur cube in front still gets fed (26.3), droppers still drop, and nothing is placed into someone else's land claim. Shown on the info page

## 1.13.24
- Server shop: claim block and command listings show their item model (set in the panel, like one from the server's resource pack) in /market

## 1.13.23
- Dashboard: server.properties, game rules and data pack settings get a Save/Cancel bar that shows once something changed, like the panel's (the panel shows it under the page)

## 1.13.22
- Players online and the Nice Actions Time/Date HUD text for the panel's Discord channel topic (panel 1.12.25)

## 1.13.21
- Chat sent to the panel carries the player's Fabled Roots race, for "[Oakhearted] Name" on Discord (panel 1.12.21)

## 1.13.20
- Claims: when a claim of an absent owner goes to a co-owner, the old owner is no longer part of it (they stayed on as co-owner in 1.13.19)

## 1.13.19
- Claims: when the owner has been away longer than the set days, the claim (with its subclaims) goes to its first co-owner who played within that time, and the old owner stays on as co-owner; only claims without such a co-owner are removed. The new owner doesn't pay claim blocks for it

## 1.13.18
- Pets in the Server shop and the pet shop say [ Buy ] like the other listings, or [ View ] for a pet you already have

## 1.13.17
- Market: the seller name of the Server shop's listings can be set in the panel (default "the server"); it shows on the listings and in the sales

## 1.13.16
- Market: pets for sale are listed in the Server shop too (their page's Back goes back there); the separate Pets button in /market is gone (/pets still opens the pet shop)
- Claim block and command listings can be switched off without deleting them ("For sale", like pets)

## 1.13.15
- Market: a Server shop in /market, apart from the player listings (Browse is now "Player listings")
  - Claim blocks are sold there instead of in their own shop in the claim menu ("Buy claim blocks" in /claim opens the Server shop): any number of listings, each with its own blocks, price, icon and optional end, paid in the market currency. The old claim shop settings are dropped; set the listings again in the panel
  - Command listings: run console commands for the buyer ({player}, {uuid}, {x}, {y}, {z}, {dimension}), with a name, description, icon, price, optional end and per-player limit
  - Server sales are kept with the market's sales (seller "the server")

## 1.13.14
- With the Nice Actions data pack: its quick actions menu (G) gets buttons for Land Claims (/claim), Pets (/pets) and the Market (/market), for whichever of them is on when the server starts. The data pack itself isn't changed

## 1.13.13
- Pets: "Random pet" in /pets (for players with two or more pets): a different one of your pets every time you teleport, die or take it out, like Random moves and particles. Taking out one pet switches it off

## 1.13.12
- Pets: each pet has its own head size (set in the panel); pets from before keep the size all pets had

## 1.13.11
- Pets can have a description (set in the panel): shown on the pet's page in the shop and under each pet in /pets

## 1.13.10
- Market: if your inventory changes while the sell menu is open (something used up or picked up), listing asks you to pick the item again instead of listing a different item at your price
- Claims: flint and steel, spawn eggs, item frames and the like can't put things inside someone else's claim from just outside its edge
- Tree felling no longer breaks nether wart blocks or mangrove roots inside a claim you can't build in
- GOML import: GOML's other owners become builders like trusted players (the owner can make co-owners later)

## 1.13.9
- Pets ride along with their owner in every player's game, so they stay right with you at any speed (no more dragging behind and flinging forward) and move smoothly; their moves and turns glide in between. Lazy still waits in place and catches up on its own
- Saplings planting themselves aren't stopped by plants any more: they take the place of grass, ferns, flowers, bushes, leaf litter and the like (what a growing tree would grow over), and when their spot is taken (a slab, a block) they take root right next to it
- Saplings: a chance (in percent) that a sapling plants itself at all, decided once per sapling

## 1.13.8
- Saplings planting themselves work more reliably: also on mud, soul soil, paths and other blocks an item sinks into a little, and when the sapling lies on the edge of a block
- Saplings only plant themselves on natural ground (dirt, grass, podzol, mycelium, moss, mud and clay for mangroves), not on farmland; the list can be changed in the panel
- Pets no longer zip back and forth when you run or fly: they follow an evened-out copy of your movement
- Pet particles appear around the head and float where they appear (no push of their own), and much less often
- Honey drips (each drop played a dripping sound), Crits and Sparks are gone from the particle choices; pets that had them have no particles now
- Portal uses the purple particles that drift up from a crying obsidian or respawn anchor (reverse portal)
- New pet particles: Autumn Leaves (red, orange and yellow), Lush Leaves (green), Witch, Ominous, Raid Omen, Campfire Smoke and Noxious Gas (26.2 and newer); the smoke and gas only now and then, since they're big and slow to fade
- Random, for moves and for particles: a new pick every time you teleport, die or take your pet out
- Names of more than one word start each word with a capital (Figure Eight, Soul Flames, Cherry Petals, Pale Leaves)
- All pet moves play slower (about 60% of their old speed), Halo even more (about a fifth)

## 1.13.7
- Own link commands are added a moment after the server starts (and after /reload), once every mod has its commands; a link never takes over, or later removes, another mod's command of the same name
- /pets on and off can't fail when the pet that was out is gone
- The pet numbers for the panel are copied before they're sent, so a purchase at that moment can't break the answer
- A pet whose head can't be made is tried again once a minute instead of every tick (which filled the log)

## 1.13.6
- Own link commands from the panel, like /website, /discord or /vote, with more names (aliases): each answers with a clickable link. They're added and removed while the server runs (players' command lists update right away); a name another command already uses is skipped
- /website is one of these now instead of a fixed command (a website set before becomes the /website link), and /info's text before the link and link text can be set

## 1.13.5
- /pets grant <player> <pet> and /pets revoke <player> <pet> for operators: pet names are suggested while typing, offline players work too (permission nicecontrolcenter.pets.admin)
- /info and /website for everyone: a clickable link to the server's info page and website, both set in the panel (permission nicecontrolcenter.links.use)

## 1.13.4
- Pet particles start outside the head instead of inside it: wisps and flames rise off the top, snow and cherry petals fall from below, magic letters fly into the head, glow, sparks and fireflies float around it
- Bubbles are gone from the particle choices (they barely showed); pets that had them have no particles now
- Player card: the player's pets are boxes in a grid, so more fit at once
- Seven more ways for pets to move (nineteen in all): Wobble, Guide (floats ahead and looks back at you), Sleepy (dozes by your feet), Pendulum, Zigzag, Satellite (a tilted ring over and under you) and Dart (jumps to a new spot every few seconds)
- Eleven more particles (twenty-two in all): Fireworks, Totem, Souls, Portal, Nautilus, Spores, Ash, Honey drips, Pale leaves, Crits and Happy

## 1.13.3
- Pets stay with their players for good: they never run out (pets bought with a time limit before are kept for good too). Instead, the shop can sell a pet until a set day.
- The player card gives a pet for good; Add days and Forever are gone
- /pets and the pet shop no longer mention how long a pet is yours, and a pet you have shows a single "You have it" line
- Market and pet menus: the dark grey text is a lighter grey, easier to read

## 1.13.2
- /pets on and /pets off: take your pet out or put it away quickly (on brings back the one you had out last)

## 1.13.1
- Pets:
  - the head faced backwards; it looks the right way now
  - stays right with you when you run, sprint or fly (before, it fell behind)
  - /pets is simpler: your pets with Take out / Put away on the front page, and buttons for Moves and Particles that count for whichever pet is out
  - seven more ways to move: Shoulder, Lazy, Peek, Halo, Spiral, Figure eight and Wander (twelve in all)
  - particle trails that rise off the pet: Wisps (end rod), Hearts, Notes, Flames, Soul flames, Snow, Glow, Cherry petals, Magic, Sparks, Fireflies, Bubbles (can be switched off for everyone in the panel)
  - a pet's new name in the panel now reaches the players who have it, like its head did
  - pets taken out of the shop stay listed in the panel while players have them, so their name and head can still be changed
  - the player card in Players → Players shows the player's pets: give one (free, for days or forever), add time, make one permanent, take it out or put it away, change how it moves and its particles, or take it away
- Market and pet shop menus look tidier: clickable [ Buy ] / [ View ] labels instead of underlined names, the facts of an item in one line, and a cleaner item page (it no longer says "You have free" when you carry no diamonds)
- Claiming land too far out says so plainly: "You can't claim here: land this far out is reset from time to time."

## 1.13.0
- Pets: floating player heads that follow their owner, only for looks (no effects, can't be hit or pushed), and every player nearby sees them (normal game clients work)
  - /pets: your pets: take one out, put it away, and pick how it moves: float next to your shoulder, orbit your head, bounce, spin above your head or follow behind you
  - Pet shop (in /pets and as Pets in /market): pets set up in the panel with a name, a head texture, a price in the market's currency (or free), how long they last (or forever) and how many can be sold (or no limit); buying a timed pet again adds more time, and a first pet comes out right away
  - Pets that run out disappear with a message; pets hide while their owner is invisible or spectating
  - Permission nicecontrolcenter.pets.use (everyone by default)
  - New API /api/pets/state for the panel

## 1.12.3
- Tool wear multipliers fixed:
  - silk touch spawners only ever took the normal 1 point, because the game wears the tool after the break event;
  - tree felling and vein mining now also scale the wear of the block you break yourself (before, only the extra logs and ores were scaled, so a small tree or vein barely showed the multiplier).

## 1.12.2
- @Mentions also work in messages sent from the panel's Chat tab: the mentioned player hears the plop, and the mention is coloured when that's switched on
- Bone meal on more plants shows the green sparkles like vanilla bone meal (before, only the sound came)
- Silk touch spawners: which tools may pick spawners up can be set (pickaxes by default), and Silk Touch can be made optional

## 1.12.1
- Tool wear multipliers for tree felling, vein mining and silk touch spawners go up to 100 (was 10)

## 1.12.0
- New gameplay tweaks (all off until switched on in the panel, normal game clients work):
  - Grindstone to books: an enchanted item and a book give an enchanted book with the item's enchantments (curses stay on the item), for levels (per enchantment level, with a least and a most); the price shows above the hotbar
  - Silk touch spawners: a spawner mined with Silk Touch drops as an item that keeps its mob and places again for normal players; mobs can be excluded, with a tool wear multiplier and optional XP
  - Saplings plant themselves: saplings lying on the ground plant themselves after a set time where they can grow, keeping room around them and (optionally) off claimed land
  - Bone meal on more plants: sugar cane, cactus (up to a set height), nether wart, lily pads, vines and small flowers
  - Loyalty tridents come back from the void
  - @Mentions in chat: the mentioned player hears the plop and sees who mentioned them (also from Discord); the mention can be coloured
- Tree felling and vein mining share their tool wear code with spawners

## 1.11.2
- Tab list header and footer update every second instead of every 5 seconds, so {time}, {tps} and {ping} keep up

## 1.11.1
- Nether portals in any shape use the same frame blocks as normal portals: on 26.3 the block tag minecraft:nether_portal_frame (so blocks a data pack adds to it work for custom shapes too), on 26.1 and 26.2 obsidian

## 1.11.0
- Anvil: "never Too Expensive" now charges the full cost however high it is (it used to lower it to a limit). Game clients can't show more than 39, so the anvil shows 39 and the real price appears above the hotbar; taking the result checks and charges the real price
- Tree felling: the tree tips over away from the player and falls (block displays, seen by normal clients); its logs, crown and drops land where it falls. Can be switched off
- Tree felling and vein mining: tool wear per extra block can be set (1 = normal, 0.5 = half, 0 = none, 2 = double)
- Felling and vein mining show the breaking of every block to the player too
- Tab list: an empty line between the header/footer and the players, and optionally each player's dimension after their name, with a name and colour per dimension
- Market: Browse shows every listing with its item icon and the item's own tooltip (enchantments, lore…); click a name to buy. Search, order and pages are above the listings, Back is its own button at the bottom (also in Sell, My listings, Mailbox and the listing itself). My listings and the mailbox show icons too
- Claim and market messages come with the little chicken-egg "plop", like the Explorer's Eden data packs

## 1.10.0
- Gameplay tweaks, all set in the panel (Tweaks) and working with normal game clients:
  - Anvil: never "Too Expensive!" (costs over the limit, 39 by default, are lowered to it) and cheap renaming (1 level by default)
  - Nether portals in any shape: any closed obsidian frame (circles, arches…) lights, up to a size limit, and stays lit while the frame is whole; rectangles work as always
  - Tree felling like FallingTree: break one log with an axe and the whole tree comes down (all at once or one log per hit from the top). Sneaking breaks just one log, the axe wears per log and stops before it breaks, logs placed without leaves around stay single blocks, leaves fall within seconds, nether wart blocks and mangrove roots go with their trees, optional drops at the first log, size limits, tool/log/leaf lists
  - Vein mining: the same for ores with a pickaxe (deepslate and normal ore are one vein)
  - Every felled or mined block is broken as if the player mined it: drops, Fortune and Silk Touch, XP, tool wear and land claims work as usual
  - AFK tag in the tab list (scoreboard teams, like Fabled Roots' races, are left alone) and a tab list header/footer with players, TPS, ping and the in-game clock
- The market (/market): players sell items for the server's currency (any amount, more than a stack is fine, all components kept) and buy part or all of a listing, in dialogs. Earnings and unsold items (after the listing time) wait in the market mailbox; sellers are told when something sells
- For other mods (Merl): the server's rules in plain words (performance tweaks, borders, claims, gameplay tweaks, the market) and the claims a player owns or is a member of

## 1.9.3
- Discord bridge (with the panel): messages data packs send to everyone with /tellraw @a, and /say and /me, go to the panel as their own event (posted when switched on in the panel)
- Fabled Roots' death messages count as deaths on Discord, with the pack's own English text (data packs' language files are read for that)

## 1.9.2
- Claims switched on or off in the panel apply right away even while the server is paused with nobody online (before, claims stayed off on the map until someone joined or the server restarted)

## 1.9.1
- Claims: minecarts with chests or hoppers and chest boats can only be opened by members with the containers role (or with the claim's switch on); before, anyone could open them
- The switch "open chests and use animals" is now "open chests and interact with entities": it always covered villagers, interaction entities and other mobs too

## 1.9.0
- Land claims, like GriefPrevention but with menus: players claim land with the claim tool (two corners), and everything inside is protected from bedrock to the sky. Off until switched on in the panel; GOML keeps working alongside
- Claim mode (/claim mode or the menu) hands out the claim tool, a marked golden hoe, and takes it away again. It can't be dropped, stored or kept; whatever was in the hand comes back
- The /claim menu (dialogs, no client mod): your claim blocks and claims. Per claim: members with a role (visitor, containers, builder, co-owner), what everyone else may do (doors, chests, PvP, explosions, fire, mob griefing), name and colour, subclaims with their own members, abandoning. Shortcuts: /claim list, trust, untrust, abandon; /claim admin bypass, delete, blocks
- Claim blocks: a start amount and more per hour of play (not while AFK), bonus blocks from admins, and an optional shop in the menu that sells blocks for an item
- Claims only inside the permanent area (soft border) and the allowed dimensions; minimum size, claims per player and removing claims of long-gone players are settings
- Borders show as fake blocks only the player sees: corners, markers along the edges, subclaims and the first corner. The blocks are settings; claim_color uses the concrete closest to the claim's colour
- Protected: building and breaking, doors, buttons, chests and other storage, buckets, animals, villagers, armor stands, frames and vehicles. Also players hurting animals (arrows too) and each other (PvP switch), arrows setting off blocks (targets, bells, buttons, TNT) or breaking frames, explosions (blocks, and animals, frames and armor stands inside), fire spread and lava lighting fires, water and lava flowing in, pistons pushing in, endermen, the wither, ravagers, zombies breaking doors and trampling farmland
- Claims are kept in the world folder (nicecontrolcenter-claims.json); the panel reads, edits and copies them through the dashboard API, and they show on the web map in their colour

## 1.8.0
- Fix: with the activation range on, mobs far from players stopped ticking entirely instead of every few ticks (a skipped mob's own tick count doesn't move; the turn now follows the world's clock)
- Mob limits per chunk: pick mobs or groups (all animals, all monsters, villagers, water mobs) and how many may live in one chunk; used by the cleanup tool, the schedule's cleanup step and the new auto caps, which apply them every few seconds (optionally also a dropped item limit, oldest first). Named, tamed, leashed and persistent mobs are never removed
- Mob pushing cap (Paper's max-entity-collisions): a mob pushes at most N others per tick, and the search for them stops early; cramming damage unchanged. 150 cows in a pen: 4.7 → 2.9 ms per tick
- Junk despawns sooner: chosen items (cobblestone, netherrack, rotten flesh…) after e.g. 60 seconds; arrows mobs shot after 10 seconds
- Save limits per chunk (Paper's entity-per-chunk-save-limit): only N arrows, XP orbs, snowballs… are written when a chunk saves
- Lighter mob AI: far from players, mobs re-plan their goals only every few ticks (Pufferfish's goal throttle); while lagging, a limit on new path searches per tick
- Idle hoppers wait a few ticks before looking again (moving and locked hoppers unchanged)
- Armor stands that stand still tick once a second (Paper's armor-stands-tick: false). 400 stands: 7.3 → 3.6 ms
- Lag protection has two more steps before the view distance: activation ranges halved, then entities shown from 60 % of the distance
- Chunks loaded without players (forceload, ender pearls, portals, dragon) with why, whether they tick and what's there, and Release
- Players table: measured tick time around each player (each chunk counted for the nearest player), their share of the total and their costliest chunk; sorted by it

## 1.7.3
- The panel's Commands page: every command with the mod that adds it, the lowest operator level that may use it, its permission node (fabric-permissions-api checks) and how it's written
- Commands run in the panel's console can be shown to online operators, grey and italic like vanilla's notices ("[Admin ┃ NiceRon: /time set day]"); switched on in the panel

## 1.7.2
- The changes of 1.7.1 under a new number, so panels install them: "Admin ┃ <account>: …" for messages from the panel (1.7.1 was rebuilt after its first release, and panels only update to higher numbers)
- The dashboard on its own gets the panel's look: brand glow and block grid, each page in its colour (tabs and card icons), gradient buttons

## 1.7.1
- Item tooltips on the Players page show names and lore with their colours and styles (§ codes and coloured text no longer show as plain text); lore shows like in the game
- Player cards show when the player first joined and when they were last online (or "online since"); kept in the world folder (nicecontrolcenter-seen.json), with an estimate from file dates for players from before
- GOML claims show on the map (and player cards) within seconds: they're read from GOML's memory instead of the saved world, which only changed on the next world save; both of GOML's save formats are understood
- Messages from the panel read "Admin ┃ <name>: …" with the sender's panel account, private ones and chat to everyone alike (chat no longer goes out as /say); message_prefix takes {user}; the old defaults "[Admin]" and "Admin:" move to the new one, a prefix you set yourself stays
- Waypoint names and descriptions on the Players page are read correctly when stored as text components

## 1.7.0
- Performance tools in the spirit of Paper and ServerCore, set up in the panel (config/nicecontrolcenter-performance.json, applied live):
  - Dynamic distances: while ticks take too long, the simulation distance, then the mob caps, then the view distance step down; they go back up once the server is calm
  - Entity activation range: mobs far from every player tick only every few ticks (never falling, swimming, fighting, burning, leashed, riding or raiding ones)
  - Larger merge radius for dropped items and XP, breeding caps for animals and villagers, villagers stuck in trading cells think less often
  - Frozen chunks: nothing ticks in them until they're unfrozen
- Cleanup on demand: dropped items and XP, or mobs over a per-chunk cap (never named, tamed, leashed or persistent ones), with a preview of what would go
- Maintenance mode (config/nicecontrolcenter-maintenance.json): only operators and staff can join, its own server list message and icon (only in the list: server.properties is left alone), a countdown in chat before everyone else is kicked
- The in-game date and time (Nice Actions calendar) is sent along with the map data, for the panel's public map
- The soft border shows as green particles to players near it, a round hard border as red ones ("Show soft border" in the panel's borders)
- Players put back from past the hard border bring their vehicle (and whoever rides it), leashed animals and following pets
- Waypoint names and descriptions stored as text components are read correctly for the map, with the owner's UUID for their head
- Charts use the Inter font

## 1.6.0
- Pregeneration carries on after a restart: the running job and the queue are kept in config/nicecontrolcenter-pregen.json and pick up at the ring they were working on (Stop clears them)
- Pregeneration can slow down (default) or pause while players are online, or keep full speed
- Lag alerts also go to the panel (for its Discord alerts)

## 1.5.0
- Pregeneration runs on its own thread instead of in the server tick: it only queues chunks for Minecraft's world-generation threads, sleeps until one is done and still backs off when ticks get long; counting a huge area no longer holds up the server
- Pregeneration can skip an inner area (only the outer wilds) and several dimensions can be queued one after another
- Borders per dimension (config/nicecontrolcenter-borders.json, set in the panel's World tab): a soft border around the permanent area and a hard border nobody gets past; players past the hard border (walking, flying, pearls, portals) are put back where they last stood inside, circles included; a square hard border also sets that dimension's world border
- New Disk tile with the stat tiles: size of the world, of the whole server folder and free space on the drive (measured every 2 minutes)
- Pregeneration holds each chunk with its own ticket until it's fully generated; before, a few percent of chunks could be unloaded half-way and counted as failed
- The GC tile also shows how busy the collector is in the background ("busy N%"); concurrent collectors like ZGC barely pause, so with a nearly full heap the tile showed 0% paused while the server was waiting for memory. It turns gold or red then, and the memory pressure finding says so

## 1.4.5
- New keep_reports_days setting (default 14): recordings and reports older than that are deleted at server start, after each new report and once an hour; 0 keeps them forever

## 1.4.4
- Dashboard links are renewed at every server start by default; the new token_hours setting keeps a link working for that many hours instead (also across restarts), then makes a new one
- The stat cards are back to their previous look and sit in a second row of the pinned top bar (one row that scrolls sideways on phones)
- The Explorer's Eden icon for home-screen shortcuts (apple-touch-icon), like on the website

## 1.4.3
- Item tooltips in the player inspector show right away when hovering, drawn like the game's (name, enchantments, item id)
- The stat tiles keep following the server live when the browser's clock differs from the server's (another machine or a container); before, they could fall back to slower window values

## 1.4.2
- The stat tiles (TPS, MSPT, CPU, memory, GC, entities, block entities, chunks, players) are pinned in the top bar and stay in view while scrolling, also inside the panel; details are in each tile's tooltip, and less important tiles step aside on narrower screens
- The live badge only shows the connection state (Live, paused, disconnected), no TPS

## 1.4.1
- The Chat tab is a copy of the in-game chat: everything players get in their chat window, with Minecraft's colors and formatting (bold, italic, underline, links), including tellraw, /msg, command feedback, joins, leaves, deaths, advancements and Discord messages
- A message sent to several players at once is one line; messages only some players got show who got them (→ names) and can be hidden with "Private messages"
- Messages to everyone while nobody is online (/say, Discord) are kept too

## 1.4.0
- New Chat tab in the dashboard: player chat, /say and /me, joins, leaves, deaths and advancements, and messages from Discord (Discord: JustSync, or the panel's Discord bridge), with filters, search and a box to send a message to everyone (as /say)
- The console no longer has its own Chat filter; chat lives in the Chat tab

## 1.3.3
- The stat tiles always show the server right now: MSPT as min / median / 95%ile / max of the last 10 seconds, GC over the last 10 seconds, and no window averages underneath
- The stat tiles are on every tab, not only the Overview

## 1.3.2
- The player inspector's list fills the window height and stays in view while a long player detail scrolls

## 1.3.1
- Pregeneration no longer waits for each chunk on the server thread (a large area could stall a tick until the watchdog stopped the server); chunks are now requested in the background, about 250 per second on a fast machine
- Pregeneration keeps the server awake without writing pause-when-empty-seconds to server.properties, so a crash during a run can't leave it at 0
- Updates from GitHub (update_github) pick the release built for the server's Minecraft version instead of GitHub's "latest" release, which could be a jar for another version

## 1.3.0
- One jar each for Minecraft 26.3, 26.2 and 26.1 (26.1, 26.1.1 and 26.1.2)
- The stat tiles size to their content: MSPT and Memory get more room than the single numbers, so nothing is cut off (one row on wide screens, two rows in between, two per row on phones)
- With the panel: pregeneration of a square or circle (Chunky-style spiral, only as fast as the server has room for, keeps going while the server would otherwise pause when empty)
- With the panel: the web map's overlays (players, world border, GOML claims, waypoint hubs) and the map colors of all blocks, modded ones included

## 1.2.0
- With the Nice Control Center Panel: the Discord bridge's login check (players need a linked Discord account when the panel asks for it) and chat, join, leave, death and advancement messages for Discord
- "Error sending packet clientbound/minecraft:disconnect" and "handleDisconnection() called twice" are marked as harmless (connections closing during a server-list ping or a failed login)

## 1.1.0
- Works with the Nice Control Center Panel (Docker): when the panel runs the server, the dashboard listens only inside the container, the panel shows it in its Dashboard tab with its own accounts and permissions, and /ncc web links open the panel
- The dashboard follows the panel's light/dark switch and drops its own header when shown inside the panel

## 1.0.6
- The live TPS badge in the header keeps its width when the value changes (e.g. 20.0 → 19.9)

## 1.0.5
- The TPS, MSPT, CPU, Memory, entity, chunk and player tiles show the current second (updated every second) instead of the chosen time window; the window's average is the small line underneath. GC stays a window value
- Charts scroll smoothly out at the left edge instead of the oldest part being cut off

## 1.0.4
- Answers to admin messages pop up as a notification on every tab (click to open the conversation), with the count in the browser tab title
- Player inspector: the last grave shows who looted it (highlighted if it wasn't the owner) or that it expired, instead of just "removed"

## 1.0.3
- The Memory tile is laid out like MSPT: in use after GC, with garbage not collected yet (both color-coded), and the maximum

## 1.0.2
- \n typed into the MOTD in the server.properties editor is saved as a real second line instead of the text "\n"
- New GC tile next to Memory: share of time paused for garbage collection, number of pauses and the average pause
- "Close to its limit" only shows when more than 2% of ticks run over budget, not because of a handful of slow ticks (autosaves) while the server keeps up easily

## 1.0.1
- Packs installed as mods show their real name in data pack settings and elsewhere (Warping Wonders instead of "wawo")
- Data pack settings also find packs that keep their settings in their own storage, like Katters Structures
- The server name in the dashboard and in reports shows the MOTD's colors and formatting instead of the raw § codes

## First release
- Live monitor that starts with the server: TPS, MSPT (min/med/95%ile/max), CPU, memory, entities, chunks and players for the last 1, 5, 15 and 60 minutes
- "What's costing performance": plain-language findings with the cause and a tip, all in ms per tick
- Data pack functions timed down to single command lines, mods found with a stack sampler, entity and block entity types, busiest chunks and lag spikes with their causes
- World generation cost per step, structure and feature, grouped by data pack or mod
- Player view, redstone clock finder, settings advisor and bloat check
- Lag alerts in chat and automatic lag reports, plus manual recordings as shareable HTML reports and a before/after comparison
- Data pack settings from in-game settings menus, editable in the dashboard (same options and limits as the menu)
- In-game date and time in the dashboard header (Nice Actions calendar or the Minecraft day counter)
- Web dashboard in the Explorer's Eden style with light and dark mode, /ncc commands, an in-game boss bar and LuckPerms permission nodes
- Errors & warnings from the server log, grouped and credited to data packs and mods, with a chat message after a /reload that broke something
- Console in the dashboard: live log and a command line
- Automatic updates for mods and data packs from Modrinth and GitHub, installed at restart, with backups and rollback (/ncc updates)
- Dashboard split into tabs
- Server resource pack kept up to date from a link, GitHub or Modrinth, with the SHA-1 worked out by the mod
- server.properties editor in the dashboard
- Live player inspector: online and offline players, inventories, homes, graves, waypoints and claims
- Gamerule editor in the dashboard
- Data pack settings laid out like the packs' in-game menus, with search
- In-game settings screen through Mod Menu (singleplayer)
- Smoother live charts and in-game clock; calmer layout with a compact header on every tab but the Overview
- Scheduled commands: once, daily, on weekdays or every few minutes, several commands per task
- Private messages with players (they answer with /nccreply), ban and unban in the player inspector
- Scheduled tasks can be chained (run after another task, with a delay), and commands can be spaced out with wait lines or a pause
- Player inspector shows each player's client and recognised mods, and an x-ray hint from mining statistics
